<configuration name="acl.conf" description="Network Lists">
  <network-lists>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      NAT DETECTION

      100.64.0.0/10 is RFC 6598 Shared Address Space (carrier-grade
      NAT). It used to be listed as "deny" here alongside the RFC1918
      ranges — which was correct for a cloud deployment reachable over
      the public internet, and is WRONG for this one.

      Jio delivered this trunk as a dedicated circuit, and every address
      on it lives inside 100.64.0.0/10:
        our SIP endpoint  100.65.188.4
        circuit gateway   100.65.188.1
        Jio SBC           100.64.0.4
        Jio media         100.64.0.6

      Treating our own peer as a NAT'd endpoint makes sofia rewrite SDP
      against a NAT that isn't there, and the classic symptom is a call
      that connects with silence in one or both directions. So the Jio
      circuit ranges are explicitly excluded from NAT handling below,
      while the rest of CGNAT space stays denied.
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <list name="nat.auto" default="allow">
      <node type="deny" cidr="192.168.0.0/16"/>
      <node type="deny" cidr="10.0.0.0/8"/>
      <node type="deny" cidr="172.16.0.0/12"/>
      <node type="deny" cidr="127.0.0.0/8"/>
      <!-- Jio circuit — direct peers, NOT behind NAT -->
      <node type="deny" cidr="100.65.188.0/28"/>
      <node type="deny" cidr="100.64.0.0/24"/>
      <!-- Remaining CGNAT space is still treated as NAT'd -->
      <node type="deny" cidr="100.64.0.0/10"/>
    </list>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      INBOUND TRUNK ACL — the toll-fraud guard.

      Only Jio's SBC and media hosts may send us SIP. Everything else
      is denied. On a circuit this is belt-and-braces (there is no
      internet path to port 5060 in the first place) but it costs
      nothing and protects against anything else on the LAN.

      Sources, from the Jio provisioning sheet:
        SBC IP    100.64.0.4   — SIP signalling
        Media IP  100.64.0.6   — RTP
        Gateway   100.65.188.1 — circuit gateway

      NOTE: /32 host entries are deliberate. Jio named single hosts,
      not ranges. If calls are rejected with "IP xxx not in list", get
      the full CIDR from Jio rather than widening this to a guess.
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <list name="heynikki-sip-trunks" default="deny">
      <node type="allow" cidr="100.64.0.4/32"/>
      <node type="allow" cidr="100.64.0.6/32"/>
      <node type="allow" cidr="100.65.188.1/32"/>
      <!-- Our own /28 — covers the circuit gateway and any Jio-side
           host in the delivered pool. -->
      <node type="allow" cidr="100.65.188.0/28"/>
    </list>

  </network-lists>
</configuration>
